# Narrow, fingerprint-scoped gitleaks suppressions — never a wildcard/path-wide
# ignore. See scripts/audit-public-repository and KNOWN_ISSUES.md for the audit
# process this supports.
#
# This file is intentionally EMPTY of fingerprints.
#
# It previously carried one, pinned to a commit in this repository's earlier
# history: tests/unit/test_config_schema.py's
# test_cli_scan_secrets_detects_planted_secret writes a synthetic,
# non-functional AWS-access-key-shaped string ("AKIA" followed by the
# sequential alphabet — never a real or activatable key) # gitleaks:allow
# to a scratch file, asserts the secret scanner detects it, then deletes the
# file in a `finally` block. That fixture predated the `gitleaks:allow`
# convention, and gitleaks scans each historical commit's own diff
# independently, so a marker added in a later commit could not retroactively
# clear the earlier one — hence a fingerprint.
#
# Every occurrence of that fixture, in code and in the documentation that
# discusses it, now carries an inline `gitleaks:allow` marker on its own line
# (inside an HTML comment in the markdown files, so it stays invisible when
# rendered). This repository's history begins at the commit that introduced
# those markers, so no historical commit remains to suppress.
#
# Prefer an inline marker at the source over a fingerprint here: a fingerprint
# is opaque at the point where a reader actually meets the string, and it
# silently stops matching as soon as the line moves.
